  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None; the full paper text, including appendices, is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study uses sparse autoencoders as an interpretability tool on single-cell foundation models. It does not test superposition or the listed claims about LLM feature geometry or capacity.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports SAE feature associations in single-cell foundation models (scFMs), not LLMs or toy networks. These results do not measure the behavior named by V1–V6. They are recorded under fits_no_version. The feature-steering results concern batch integration and are not evidence for the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses BatchTopK SAEs as a tool to interpret single-cell foundation models (scGPT, scFoundation). It reports feature–label associations (AMI/F1), enrichment and steering effects on batch integration. It never tests superposition: no toy models, no polysemanticity or capacity analysis, no comparison of neuron versus SAE-feature interpretability, and no feature dimensionality test. The subjects are not LLMs, so V4's scope (real LLMs) is not met. Its cross-study generalization finding touches on SAE-feature canonicity (V5) only loosely, and the authors attribute it partly to the model, not the SAE.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that SAEs reveal biologically meaningful and technical features in pre-trained scFMs, and that encoding differs by model. Cell-type features often fail to unify across studies, and removing batch features improves batch integration, showing causal relevance. They add that SAEs are substantially harder to apply here than in language models, and that steering is probably not yet a useful batch-correction method.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains BatchTopK SAEs on intermediate scFM token representations and analyzes biological and technical associations; it does not use toy networks or test superposition geometry.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Feature interpretation and generalization vary by model, dataset, and cell-type granularity; fine-tuning did not noticeably improve feature AMI or F1 on the COVID-19 dataset.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "BatchTopK SAEs on layer-10 token activations of pre-trained scGPT and scFoundation yield features associated with cell types, disease status and sequencing technology. Association strength varies by feature and model (AMI 0.11–1.00).",
        "proposed_claim": "SAEs trained on single-cell foundation model activations recover sparse features aligned with biological and technical variables (analogue of V4 outside LLMs).",
