      {
        "finding": "About one third of 100 annotated CellxGene features were hard to interpret. Cell-type features often aligned better with study-specific subsets than with full cell-type labels (Fig. 3). The authors say this may partly reflect SAE limitations.",
        "proposed_claim": "In scFMs, SAE features do not always form unified, generalizable concepts; interpretability is incomplete (loosely related to V5 outside LLMs).",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Clamping batch-associated SAE features improved batch-integration scores for fine-tuned scGPT on all three datasets (e.g., Pancreas total 0.50 to 0.69). Pre-trained steering worked consistently only on Pancreas.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE features in scFMs are causally involved in model outputs and can be steered.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The paper says many features are difficult to interpret and automated interpretation is limited. Feature generalization across studies is variable, and the authors note SAE limitations as a possible explanation. The standard SAE loss-recovered metric was not usable because scFM training loss was consistently high; the authors instead introduce an Embedding Recovery Score.",
    "rows_left_out": "Steering and batch-integration benchmark results in Table 2, Tables 16–17, and Figures 4 and 9 were left out because they measure batch-integration outcomes, not any behavior named by V1–V6.",
    "untested": [
